      "source": "Residential Tenancies Board",
      "summary": "RTB describes deduction grounds including arrears, tenant-responsible bills and damage beyond ordinary wear.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "sourceDocumentId": "10e638cb-94c7-597a-89a9-bb2d1bb7b79d",
          "sourceUrl": "https://rtb.ie/renting/rights-responsibilities/security-deposits/",
          "documentTitle": "Security deposits",
          "documentType": "rtb_guidance",
          "date": null,
          "pageNumber": null,
          "sectionHeading": "Reasons for keeping the deposit",
          "checksum": "951d4b042870a131d017b70898adbae845786bd8c85bfae22e935ec8dea20525",
          "rawStart": 1956,
          "rawEnd": 2605,
          "chunkId": "10e638cb-94c7-597a-89a9-bb2d1bb7b79d:1956-2605",
          "originalText": "Reasons for keeping the deposit\n\nA landlord can keep all or part of the deposit for:\n\nRent arrears: if you owe rent at the end of the tenancy, the landlord can deduct the amount you owe from the deposit.\n\nUnpaid bills: if you have not paid utility bills that you are responsible for under the lease or tenancy agreement, the deposit can be used to cover the unpaid bills.\n\nDamage: above normal wear and tear such as broken windows, holes in walls, leaving the property in an unhygienic or unsafe condition, missing or broken items.\n\nInsufficient notice: if you don’t give the correct notice or end a fixed-term tenancy early, deductions can apply.\n\n"
        }
      ]
    },
